<div class="how-to-play-dialog scrollable os-primer">
    <div class="os-primer-grid">
        <section class="text-container-300 htp-game-runs-on-tags">
            <h3>This Game Runs on Tags</h3>
            <p>Tags are short descriptors that establish what’s true and add to or subtract from the Power of your character’s actions. Examples include <mark class="tag">streetwise</mark>, <mark class="tag">ghost-sight</mark>, <mark class="weakness">glitchy cyberarm</mark>, and <mark class="status">hexed-2</mark>.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-300 htp-themes">
            <h3>Themes</h3>
            <p>Your character has four themes. Each includes:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Power tags.</strong> Positive tags that add Power to your actions, including the theme’s title tag. A burned power tag is unusable until recovered.</li>
                <li><strong>Weakness tags.</strong> Negative tags that reduce the Power of your actions. When one is invoked, mark <strong>Upgrade</strong> on its theme.</li>
                <li><strong>Motivation.</strong> Something your character seeks, defends, or upholds through this theme.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-300 htp-story-tags">
            <h3>Story Tags</h3>
            <p>Story tags are temporary and can help or hinder depending on the situation. Examples include <mark class="tag">spoofed access badge</mark> and <mark class="tag">rooftop lookout</mark>.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Remove a story tag when it is <strong>burned</strong>.</li>
                <li>Story tags can expire over time.</li>
                <li>Record personal story tags in your <strong>Loadout</strong> and general story tags on the <strong>Tracking Card</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-300 htp-statuses">
            <h3>Statuses</h3>
            <p>Statuses describe conditions with a tier from 1 to 6, such as <mark class="status">pinned down-3</mark> or <mark class="status">dialed in-2</mark>. Record the status and its tier on a Tracking Card.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Stacking.</strong> If you gain the same or a similar status again, mark the new tier. If that box is already marked, mark the box to its right. The highest marked box is the current tier.</li>
                <li><strong>Reducing.</strong> Move all marks left by the number of tiers reduced. Remove any marks that fall below tier 1.</li>
                <li><strong>Limits.</strong> Tier 5 overcomes your character. Tier 6 kills or transforms them.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-300 htp-hero-development">
            <h3>Character Development</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Upgrade.</strong> Mark Upgrade when one of your weakness tags is invoked. At 3 Upgrade, the theme gains an upgrade, usually a new power tag.</li>
                <li><strong>Decay.</strong> Mark Decay when you ignore or betray your Motivation. At 3 Decay, replace the theme.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-300 htp-fellowship">
            <h3>The Crew</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Power tags.</strong> Single-use. Burn them when invoked and recover them during <strong>Downtime</strong>. They cannot be <strong>burned for Power</strong>.</li>
                <li><strong>Weakness tags.</strong> Mark <strong>Upgrade</strong> on the Crew theme when one is invoked.</li>
                <li><strong>Relationship tags.</strong> Single-use story tags, positive or negative, between characters. Recreate them during Downtime.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-long-paper-background htp-during-a-scene">
            <h3>During a Scene</h3>
            <p>Repeat <strong>Establish → Action → Consequences</strong> until the stakes are resolved or the scene ends. Then move to the next scene or to <strong>Downtime</strong>.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Establish.</strong> The <strong>MC</strong> describes the scene, highlights the stakes, introduces Challenges, and makes Threats. The MC asks “What do you do?” and gives a different character the spotlight each turn.</li>
                <li><strong>Action.</strong> The character in the spotlight describes what they do. The MC chooses how to resolve it. A <strong>Simple</strong> action is the MC’s call, a <strong>Quick</strong> action calls for a roll, and a <strong>Detailed</strong> action calls for a roll and spending Power.</li>
                <li><strong>Consequences.</strong> The MC can deliver Consequences when a Threat is ignored, a roll produces Consequences, or the MC invokes a character’s weakness tag. In the last case, the character marks <strong>Upgrade</strong>. Consequences can change the fiction, give or burn tags, or give or reduce statuses. The character may be able to react.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-300 htp-challenges">
            <h4>Challenges</h4>
            <p>Challenges are adversaries, obstacles, and dangers that put your character or their goals at risk. They act through <strong>Threats</strong> and <strong>Consequences</strong>.</p>
            <p>The MC can resolve a Challenge with a <strong>Quick</strong> success or require a <strong>Detailed</strong> action that brings a status up to one of its Limits, such as <mark class="limit">disable 4</mark>, <mark class="limit">banish 2</mark>, or <mark class="limit">expose 3</mark>.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-500 htp-making-a-roll">
            <h4>Making a Roll</h4>
            <p><strong>Count the action’s Power.</strong> Invoke relevant tags and statuses from your character, the target, or the environment.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Add 1 Power per positive tag and subtract 1 per negative tag.</li>
                <li>Add 3 Power when you <strong>burn one of your character’s positive tags for Power</strong>.</li>
                <li>Add the tier of the strongest positive status and subtract the tier of the strongest negative status.</li>
                <li><strong>Apply Scale whenever there’s a Scale difference.</strong> Scale runs from 0 to 6. Treat the difference as a status that always applies to the roll. It helps when your side has the greater Scale and hinders when the opposing side does. Equal Scale adds no modifier.</li>
                <li>Mark <strong>Upgrade</strong> for each invoked weakness tag.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Roll 2d6 and add Power.</strong></p>
            <div class="os-primer-outcomes">
                <div class="roll-result-success"><strong>10+ or double sixes</strong><span>Success</span><img class="os-primer-result" alt="" src="modules/otherscape-mist-skin/icons/success.svg"></div>
                <div class="roll-result-partial-success"><strong>7–9</strong><span>Success and Consequences</span><img class="os-primer-result" alt="" src="modules/otherscape-mist-skin/icons/mixed-success.svg"></div>
                <div class="roll-result-failure"><strong>6 or less or double ones</strong><span>Consequences</span><img class="os-primer-result" alt="" src="modules/otherscape-mist-skin/icons/consequences.svg"></div>
            </div>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-300 htp-spending-power">
            <h4>Spending Power</h4>
            <p>Spend Power on your action’s <strong>Effects</strong>. If your Power is zero or less, spend 1.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Add, burn, or recover a tag (2 Power)</li>
                <li>Give or reduce a status (1 Power per tier)</li>
                <li>Discover a valuable detail (1 Power)</li>
                <li>Perform an extra feat alongside other Effects (1 Power)</li>
                <li>Add a single-use tag (your last 1 Power)</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-300 htp-reaction">
            <h4>Reaction</h4>
            <p>If the <strong>MC</strong> allows a reaction, describe it, count Power, and roll as for a Detailed action. Lessening Consequences costs <strong>1 Power per status tier</strong> or <strong>2 Power per tag</strong>.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>10+ or double sixes.</strong> Spend Power + 1 on any Effect.</li>
                <li><strong>7–9.</strong> Spend Power only to lessen the Consequences.</li>
                <li><strong>6 or less or double ones.</strong> Take the Consequences as given.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="text-container-300 htp-camping">
            <h4>Downtime</h4>
            <p>When the crew takes a break, the <strong>MC</strong> may call for downtime. Go around the table and describe each character’s activity in broad strokes. Each player chooses <strong>one</strong> benefit:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Mark one Upgrade box</strong> on a personal theme or the Crew theme.</li>
                <li><strong>Spend 3 Power without rolling.</strong> Split it among healing and recovery, preparing or Loading Up, creating relationship story tags, and gathering information. Recovering a burned personal or Crew power tag costs <strong>1 Power per tag</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Players may take regular actions as time allows, but the MC may rule that doing so costs them their downtime benefit. Characters who unpack their gear can <strong>Load Off</strong>, burning all their loadout tags. The MC then starts the next scene.</p>
        </section>
    </div>
</div>
